          60
        ],
        "hookSizes": [
          2,
          4
        ],
        "colors": [
          "orange",
          "red-brown",
          "rust"
        ],
        "behavior": "Swims in the open column in enormous numbers over a few consecutive evenings. The worms do not hatch so much as disassemble, and the swimming sections are what gets eaten.",
        "where": "Mid to upper column, outgoing tide near reef and coral",
        "vulnerable": true,
        "image": "assets/bugs/palolo-worm-spawning.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "Early May into July, in sequences of four to six consecutive nights. The folk calendar pins it to the May full moon, but current strength is the better predictor and some May full moons produce nothing while some June tides are exceptional. Carry the fly only on a trip that overlaps the window, and do not plan around it.",
    "sources": [
      "https://midcurrent.com/v2/palolo-worms-and-tarpon-the-most-bizarre-hatch-in-saltwater-and-how-to-fish-it/ — accessed 2026-09-14. NOTE: Florida Keys data used as the closest available proxy; Belize-specific timing is NOT independently confirmed."
    ]
  },
